## Why This Is Asked
Tests for feature flags are essential to validate UI gating, state persistence, and how flows handle mid-flight flag changes in real user journeys.

## Key Concepts
- Feature flags and gating
- Test data seeding and API-driven state setup
- End-to-end verification of UI state and backend persistence
- Handling mid-flow toggling and UI consistency

## Code Example
```javascript
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('onboarding with feature flag', async ({ page, request }) => {
  // Seed user with onboarding flag ON
  await request.post('/api/test/setup-user', { data: { flagName: 'new-onboarding', value: true } });
  // Login and navigate
  await page.goto('/login');
  await page.fill('#email', 'user@example.com');
  await page.fill('#password', 's3cret');
  await page.click('button[type="submit"]');
  await page.waitForURL('/dashboard');

  // Verify onboarding visible
  await expect(page.locator('#onboarding-panel')).toBeVisible();
  await expect(page.locator('#onboarding-panel .step')).toHaveCount(4);

  // Complete first step
  await page.click('#step-1 .complete');
  await expect(page.locator('#step-1')).toHaveClass(/completed/);

  // Refresh and verify persistence
  await page.reload();
  await expect(page.locator('#step-1')).toHaveClass(/completed/);

  // Turn flag OFF mid-flow and verify UI hides, but backend progress remains
  await request.post('/api/test/setup-user', { data: { flagName: 'new-onboarding', value: false } });
  await page.reload();
  await expect(page.locator('#onboarding-panel')).toBeHidden();

  // Validate backend progress still retrievable
  const res = await request.get('/api/user/progress');
  expect(res.status()).toBe(200);
  const data = await res.json();
  expect(data.stepsCompleted).toBe(1);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this test to run reliably in CI with parallelization?
- What considerations would you add for multiple feature flags interacting in the same flow?

## Why This Is Asked
Streaming UI tests are a common beginner-to-intermediate challenge in E2E testing. This question assesses the ability to design a reproducible test flow that exercises real-time updates, cancel/retry semantics, and test isolation—areas that frequently cause test flakiness in practice.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end streaming validation (SSE/WebSocket)
- Progressive rendering and ordering of chunks
- Cancel and retry behavior testing
- Test data isolation with sandboxed backends
- Network conditions simulation (throttling, outages)

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright test sketch (pseudo-implementation)
test('streaming playground flow', async ({ page }) => {
  // Setup mock backend with isolated test data
  await setupTestBackend({ isolated: true });

  // User interactions
  await page.fill('[data-testid="prompt-input"]', 'Hello world');
  await page.selectOption('[data-testid="model-select"]', 'gpt-4');
  await page.fill('[data-testid="temperature"]', '0.7');

  // Start streaming and verify chunks
  const runButton = page.locator('[data-testid="run-button"]');
  await runButton.click();

  // Verify progressive rendering
  await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="output-pane"]'))
    .toContainText('Hello', { timeout: 1000 });
  await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="output-pane"]'))
    .toContainText('Hello world', { timeout: 2000 });

  // Test cancel behavior
  await page.locator('[data-testid="cancel-button"]').click();
  await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="output-pane"]'))
    .not.toContainText('complete');

  // Test retry with different parameters
  await page.selectOption('[data-testid="model-select"]', 'gpt-3.5');
  await runButton.click();
  await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="output-pane"]'))
    .toContainText('Hello world', { timeout: 3000 });
});
```

